.TH CCOPY 1 "2024-01-15" "ctools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ccopy \- copy a conversation from one agent to a new session in another
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccopy
.RB [ \-\-dry-run ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.I source
.I destination
.br
.B ccopy
.B \-\-export-json
.I agent/session_id
.br
.B ccopy
.B \-\-import-json
.I agent
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ccopy
(context copy) copies an entire conversation from a source session into a
brand-new session in a destination agent. The source session is never modified,
so it is a true copy, not a move.
.PP
The source is
.I agent/session_id
(with no @ prefix). The destination is a bare agent name; a fresh session is
created there holding the copied conversation, and ccopy prints the command to
resume it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Show what would happen without writing anything.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Structured logging to stderr.
.TP
.BI \-\-export-json " agent/session_id"
Print the session's conversation as a JSON list of
.BR {"role", "content"}
objects on stdout, and exit. A local-pipe convenience for scripting.
.TP
.BI \-\-import-json " agent"
Read a conversation JSON from stdin, create a new session in
.IR agent ,
and print the new session id on stdout. A local-pipe convenience for scripting.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I source
The source session, as
.I agent/session_id
(e.g.
.IR opencode/ses_abc123 ),
or
.I ssh://[user@]host[:port]/agent/session_id
for a session on a remote host.
.TP
.I destination
A bare agent name (e.g.
.IR claude-code ),
or
.I ssh://[user@]host[:port]/agent
for a remote host. A new session is created in that agent.
.PP
Only agents that support session creation work as destinations
(claude-code, opencode, kilo, codex, pi). The others refuse with a clean error.
.SH "CROSS-HOST"
Either side may be remote, addressed as
.IR ssh://[user@]host[:port]/path .
ccopy runs over your normal ssh setup (keys,
.I ~/.ssh/config
, jump hosts).
.PP
The remote host does
.B not
need ctxttools installed — only
.B sshd
and a POSIX
.BR tar .
ccopy pulls the remote agent's storage (its sessions directory or its
database) over ssh with a plain
tar pipe, unpacks it into a local scratch directory, reads or writes the
conversation with the normal local agent code, and pushes any changed storage
back the same way. The storage location is anchored at the remote's $HOME
(the agent's default install root, e.g.
.IR ~/.local/share/opencode ,
.IR ~/.pi/agent ),
so the remote just needs whatever agent it already runs. Locally this reduces to:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccopy --export-json opencode/ses_abc | ccopy --import-json codex
.fi
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Copy an opencode session to a new Claude Code session:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ccopy opencode/ses_abc123 claude-code
Copied 42 message(s) from opencode/ses_abc123 to a new claude-code session: 1a2b3c
Resume it with:
  claude-code --resume 1a2b3c
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview without writing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ccopy opencode/ses_abc123 codex --dry-run
Would copy 42 message(s) from opencode/ses_abc123 to a new codex session.
.fi
.RE
.PP
Copy a conversation to a remote host:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ccopy opencode/ses_abc123 ssh://chris@remote/codex
Copied 42 message(s) from opencode/ses_abc123 to a new codex session on chris@remote: 1a2b3c
Resume it on chris@remote with:
  codex resume 1a2b3c
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Unknown agent, session not found, no conversation, or unsupported destination.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cdir (1),
.BR cextract (1),
.BR cgrep (1),
.BR cdu (1)
.SH AUTHORS
ctools project.
